# Canonical analysis run files

`Runfiles` contains the input decks for every supplied MultiPEM analysis. Each
leaf analysis directory contains `runMPEM.r`, `runMPEM_0.r`, or both:

- `runMPEM.r` performs rapid calibration or a complete joint assessment.
- `runMPEM_0.r` performs rapid new-event inference from a saved calibration
  workspace.

Read the README in the selected application and phenomenology directory before
running a deck. It records data choices, model variants, prerequisites, and
required optimization inputs.

## Directory contract

Canonical decks assume this relative layout:

```text
MultiPEM_Toolbox/
    Applications/
    Code/
    Runfiles/
        Code -> ../Code
        APPLICATION/
            Code -> ../../Applications/Code/APPLICATION_CODE
            Data -> ../../Applications/Data/APPLICATION_DATA
            PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS/runMPEM*.r
```

The symbolic links are needed only for native runs. The Docker launcher copies
the required directories into each private job snapshot.

Create the shared native link once from `Runfiles`:

```text
ln -s ../Code Code
```

Then create the application links shown in its README. Some phenomenology
directories also need a local `Code` link to prior functions.

## Native execution

From an analysis directory, run the first or only stage with:

```text
R CMD BATCH runMPEM.r runMPEM.out &
```

The trailing `&` runs the batch job in the background. For rapid analyses,
follow `runMPEM.out` and verify that it ends with `proc.time()` output and that
`.RData` exists. Then preserve a pristine calibration workspace before
starting any event stage:

```text
cp .RData .RData-calibration
```

Only after that copy is secure should you start the event stage:

```text
R CMD BATCH runMPEM_0.r runMPEM_0.out &
```

Use `tail runMPEM.out` or `tail runMPEM_0.out` to follow a background run. An
interactive alternative is `source("runMPEM.r")` from the analysis directory.

The event deck modifies and saves its working `.RData`. Retain
`.RData-calibration` even if only one event is currently planned: it is the
reusable benchmark/calibration result for future event analyses. Copy a fresh
instance to `.RData` in each event working directory.

## Optimization inputs

Multi-phenomenology calibration decks can use single-phenomenology MLE results
as starting values. Their `opt_files_in` values point to exact filenames under
a sibling `Opt` directory. On a clean checkout the directory does not exist.
Create it and copy every required file exactly as documented locally before
starting the MultiPEM deck.

These files are required by decks that hard-code `opt_files_in`; the calculation
calls `readRDS` and will fail when one is absent. Do not interpret the historical
phrase “more informed starting value” as optional for such a shipped deck unless
you also edit the deck to use `NULL` or supply `Xst` deliberately.

## Docker execution

From `../Runfiles-Docker`:

```text
Rscript mpem.R doctor
Rscript mpem.R list
Rscript mpem.R run APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS
```

Docker creates a private copy of the entire registered application. Outputs
therefore remain under the printed results directory rather than appearing in
canonical `Runfiles`.

If a later MultiPEM job needs `opt.RData` from earlier single-phenomenology
jobs, copy those files from
`RESULTS/work/APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS/opt.RData` into the canonical
`Runfiles/APPLICATION/MULTIPEM/Opt` destinations first. Create the MultiPEM job
only after staging all inputs so its snapshot contains them. See
`../Runfiles-Docker/README` for the complete lifecycle and checkpoint workflow.

## Outputs

Depending on deck settings, an analysis directory can receive:

- `.RData`, the saved workspace;
- `runMPEM.out` or `runMPEM_0.out`, the batch transcript;
- `opt.RData` or `opt_nev.RData`, optimization results; and
- sampler- or application-specific summaries printed into the transcript.

Do not commit generated output as canonical input unless a documented workflow
explicitly calls for a retained fixture. Docker jobs additionally record input
hashes, stage status, CPU adjustments, and a durable combined log.

## Adding an analysis

1. Create `Runfiles/APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS`.
2. Add at least one deck named `runMPEM.r` or `runMPEM_0.r`.
3. Keep data and code paths relative to that analysis directory.
4. Use the standard `ncores_*` variables and matching `ncor*` arguments so the
   Docker snapshot can guard worker pools.
5. Register a new application in `../Runfiles-Docker/applications.tsv`; analyses
   beneath an existing registered application are discovered automatically.
6. Run `Rscript mpem.R doctor` and `Rscript mpem.R list`, then test the new deck
   in a new results directory.

The repository README lists native packages and reproducibility guidance.
